## INTRODUCTION
As inorganic and organic hybrid materials, metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) epitomize unlimited tunability at the molecular level by judicious design of metal nodes and organic linkers.1 The new level of control towards structures and structure-related properties has led to numerous advances in the basic science, and fascinating developments with respect to applications in gas storage, separation, and catalysis.2-5 In particular, the unit cell dimensions of MOFs can be extended by elongation of organic linkers to give a series of isoreticular frameworks.6-7 The so-called isoreticular expansion of MOFs allows the systematic control of pore sizes for targeted applications in a discrete manner.8 Continuous variation of unit cell dimensions is highly desired as it promises precise control over MOF properties including the gas uptakes, pore sizes, Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas, etc. However, there has been limited success in this endeavor. Nevertheless, there are some interesting glimmers on lattice metric engineering of metal–oxide and metal–halide. For example, cesium lead halide perovskites $\mathsf{T s P b}\mathtt{X}_{3}$ , $\mathtt{X}=\mathtt{C l}.$ , Br, and I) can form mixed-anion solid solutions whose lattice parameters can be continuously tuned by adjusting halide ratio.9-10 MOFs can be loosely regarded as analogues of metal–oxide or metal– halide, which are constructed by linking cations (inorganic clusters) and anions (organic linkers). Their structural similarity inspired us to explore the availability of lattice metric engineering by adjusting the linker ratios in mixed-linker MOFs.
In fact, continuous change of unit cell parameters within a narrow range was realized in metal-halide-cluster based MOFs by varying the halide ratio.11 Besides, example of the lattice engineering by linker substitution has been demonstrated in lithium succinate, a nonporous coordination polymer.12 Partial replacement of succinates with bulkier hydroxysuccinate or methylsuccinate gradually expanded the unit cell sizes. Different from the nonporous coordination polymers, porous MOFs, with large cavity and framework rigidity, can tolerate the incorporation of bulky functional groups on linkers without altering the unit cell dimensions. A representative work by Yaghi and coworkers indicates that the MOF-5 can accommodate up to eight functionalized linkers without obvious change of backbone structure or unit cell parameters.13 Very recently, a mixed-linker Zr-MOF composing of two linear linkers with different lengths has been reported by Kim et al. in which two linkers randomly arranged in the cubic lattice.14 Similarly, mixed linker strategy has been applied in MOFs based on $\mathtt{Z n_{4}O}$ nodes, known as UMCM-8 and -9, to reduce interpenetration.15 However, fine-tuning of MOF lattice metrics in a wide range still remains a remarkably challenging endeavor to date.16 Herein, we demonstrate that the continuous variation of lattice dimension and pore size can be realized in a series of mixed-linker MOFs with various linker ratios, namely Continuously Variable MOFs (CVMOFs). Linkers with different lengths and various ratios can be homogeneously incorporated into a single CVMOF, rather than forming separate phases or domains. CVMOFs can be regarded as intermediates that bridged the gap between two singlelinker based MOFs in the traditional isoreticular expansion strategy. The unit cell volumes, BET surface areas, and pore sizes can be continuously tuned in a wide range by the variation of linker ratios in CVMOFs.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Design of CVMOFs. The mixed-linker MOF is essentially a mixed-anion solid solution in which two linkers co-exist in the lattice with one linker acting as substitutional defects of the other. Therefore, zirconium-based MOF (Zr-MOF) was selected as a matrix of CVMOFs because of their strong tolerance towards defects.17-18 Indeed, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -MOFs are known to maintain the structural intactness even if linkers are partially missing or Zr–L bonds are distorted 19-20. As expected, the reaction of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ with a pair of selected linkers gave rise to a mixed-linker CVMOF under the common synthetic condition of $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs.21 Eight linkers (hereafter named as LA to LG) with lengths ranging from 4.9 to $15.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ were selected to build CVMOFs (Figure 1). It is found that a combination of $\mathrm{LA}{+}\mathrm{LB}.$ , ${\mathrm{LB}}{+}{\mathrm{LC}},$ $\mathrm{LC+LD}_{\cdot}$ , $\mathrm{LD+LE}$ , $\mathrm{LE+LF}$ , $\mathrm{LF}{+}\mathrm{LG}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ , $\mathrm{LD+LF}$ , and $_{\mathrm{LE+LG}}$ formed mixed-linker products (CVMOF-AB, CVMOF-BC, CVMOF-CD, CVMOF-DE, CVMOF-EF, CVMOF-FG, CVMOF-DF, and CVMOFEG, respectively). However, the reaction of $\mathrm{LA}{+}\mathrm{LC}$ , $\mathtt{L B+L D}_{\mathrm{.}}$ , and $\mathrm{LC+LE}$ under identical synthetic condition gave rise to physical mixtures of two MOFs with individual linker. The relative length difference between two linkers, defined as the linkerlength difference divided by the length of shorter linker, was used to guide the synthesis of CVMOFs. Linker pairs with relative length differences below $41\%$ tend to form CVMOFs.
The successful syntheses of CVMOFs were confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). Mixed-linker CVMOFs exhibit similar PXRD patterns as the single-linker-based MOFs indicating the formation of the same crystalline phase.
However, the diffraction peak position was shifted compared to single linker-based MOFs (Figure 2a), suggesting different unit cell dimensions. The diffraction peaks of single-linkerbased MOFs were not observed in the PXRD of mixed linker CVMOFs, confirming their phase purity. The existence of two linkers and their ratios within CVMOFs were determined by the $^1\mathrm{H}$ -NMR results of digested samples (Figure S2). The synthetic conditions were carefully optimized to ensure approximately equal moles of linkers were incorporated into CVMOFs. The mole fractions of longer linkers are $53\%$ for CVMOF-AB, $68\%$ for CVMOF-BC, $48\%$ for CVMOF-CD, $40\%$ for CVMOF-DE, $57\%$ for CVMOF-EF and $85\%$ for CVMOF-FG. Furthermore, linker ratios in CVMOF-BC and CVMOF-DE as a function of linker ratios in the starting materials were plotted (Figure S3), which allow us to precisely control linker ratios in CVMOFs (Table S1). It is observed that the linker ratios in the starting materials are not always equal to the those in the products. The mole fractions of shorter linkers in CVMOFs are higher than those in the starting material, possibly due to the fact that shorter and more rigid linkers are thermodynamically favored when coordinating with $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ during CVMOF formation.
Pore Engineering of CVMOFs. The continuous change of lattice metrics promises fine tune of gas uptake capacity, pore size and BET surface area. The porosity of CVMOFs was estimated by ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar (Figures 2b and S4), and the pore size distribution was calculated from ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms using a density functional theory (DFT) model (Figure 2c). Direct activation of CVMOFs from relatively high surface tension solvents such as acetone and dichloromethane leads to reduced BET surface area. To fully activate CVMOFs, they were exchanged by n-hexane with low surface tension following the procedure reported by Matzger and coworkers.22 The total $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes, pore sizes and BET surface areas of CVMOFs were summarized in Table S2. Generally, the total ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptakes and the pore sizes of MOFs are directly related to the lengths of the linkers. The stepped increases of linker lengths in MOFs lead to a discrete change in the porosity, whereas the CVMOFs act as intermediates that bridged the gap between two single-linker based MOFs. The total ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake, pore sizes and BET surface areas of CVMOFs mostly lie in the interval of the two corresponding single-linker based MOFs except for CVMOF-BC. CVMOF-BC failed to be fully activated, as reflected by the low ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake and BET surface area. The low stability of CVMOF-BC after solvent removal is tentatively attributed to unfavored coordination mode (zigzag LC vs. linear LB) and significantly different lengths of linkers. The total ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake of CVMOFs is tuned from 157 (MOF-A) to $953\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (MOF-G) while the BET surface area increases from 585 (MOF-A) to $3791\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (MOF-G). Meanwhile, the pore size of CVMOFs continuously varies from calc. 10.9 (MOF-D) to 15.9 Å (MOF-G). It should be noted that the pore size distributions of CVMOFs are not a simple addition of two types of pores. For comparison, a physical mixture of MOF-D and MOF-E was subjected to pore size measurement by ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption, which clearly showed two pores with different sizes instead of a uniform pore (Figure S5).
Continuous Variation of Lattice Dimensions and Pore Sizes. To fine-tune the pore size, CVMOF-DE with gradually varied linker ratios are synthesized. They are denoted as CVMOF- $\mathrm{D}_{1.4}\mathrm{E}_{0.6},$ CVMOF-DE, and CVMOF- $\cdot\mathrm{D}_{0.6}\mathrm{E}_{1.4},$ where the subscript indicates the molar ratio of linkers in the starting materials. The mole fractions of LE in CVMOF-DE samples were determined by $^1\mathrm{H}$ -NMR digestion experiments to be $21\%$ , $40\%$ , and $63\%$ respectively. Microscopic images of CVMOF-DE single crystals unambiguously showed a uniform color throughout the crystals, eliminating the existence of any macroscopic domains of linkers (Figure 2d insert and S6). Since LE with azobenzene moiety has distinguishable red color whereas LD is colorless, macroscopic domains with different linker ratios are expected to show distinct colors. The peak position of PXRD gradually shifted to a low angle with the increased doping of LE (Figure 2d), suggesting a lattice expansion caused by longer LE. Pawley refinements against PXRD patterns of CVMOF-DE indicates the pure crystalline phase with a lattice parameter of $a=27.65\textrm{\AA}$ (Figure 3, Table S3), which lies in between that of MOF-D $[a=\dot{2}6.81\mathrm{\AA}^{.}$ ) and MOE-E $[a=29.47\textrm{\AA}$ ). ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms (Figure 2e) and the derived pore size distributions (Figure 2f) clearly show a steadily increased pore size by varying linker ratios. The unit-cell edge lengths, total ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptakes, pore sizes, and BET surface areas of CVMOF-DE are almost linearly related to the linker ratios, which is in line with the Vegard’s law (Figure S7). Using a similar method, CVMOFBC with gradually increased LC ratios were also synthesized, namely CVMOF- $\cdot\mathrm{B}_{1.4}\mathrm{C}_{0.6},$ CVMOF-BC, and $\mathrm{CVMOF–B}_{0.6}{\mathrm{C}}_{1.4}$ . PXRD peaks gradually shifted to low angles with the increase of LC ratio (Figure S8), demonstrating that the finetuning concept for CVMOF-DE can be more widely applicable. Theoretically, the lattice metrics related properties can be precisely controlled by adjusting the linker ratios in CVMOFs.
Insight into the Structures and Linker Distributions of CVMOFs. Direct structural evidence of CVMOFs was provided by single-crystal $\mathtt{X}$ -ray diffraction (SCXRD). Single crystal structure of CVMOF-EF can be refined, while other CVMOFs failed to form high quality single crystals suitable for SCXRD studies. To reveal the structure of CVMOFs, refined single crystal structures of MOF-E, MOF-F and CVMOF-EF were compared (Figure 4, Table S4). All of them crystallizes in the cubic space group $F m3m$ with slightly different unit cell parameters $(a=29.470(1)$ ) $\textrm{\AA}$ for MOF-E, 29.8246(11) Å for CVMOF-EF, and 30.292(11) Å for MOF-F). Within CVMOF-EF, each $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster was connected to 12 linkers into a facecentered cubic (fcu) topology. The LE and LF are substitutional disordered in the crystal lattice. Low symmetry space groups were attempted in expect to eliminate the disorder but to no avail, which excluded long-range ordered arrangement of two linkers. Importantly, the atoms in CVMOF-EF exhibited remarkably higher equivalent isotropic atomic displacement parameter $\mathrm{(U_{eq})}$ compared with MOF-E and MOF-F. For example, the $\mathrm{U_{eq}}$ of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ atoms in CVMOF-EF $(\mathrm{U_{eq}}=0.090)$ is much higher than those of MOF-E $(\mathrm{U_{eq}}=0.015)$ and MOF-F $\mathrm{(U_{eq}=}$ 0.022). This large $\mathrm{U_{eq}}$ is attributed to the static positional disorder of atoms. As two linkers with different lengths co-exist in the CVMOF-EF crystals, the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -atoms have to shift their positions to fit different linkers. Therefore, the disordered structure averaged the crystallographically observed linker lengths (13.32 Å for CVMOF-EF, $1\bar{3}.1\bar{0}\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for MOF-E, and 13.67 Å for MOF-F) and cell parameters. Because of the disorders, CVMOF-EF shows relatively weak diffraction spots in SCXRD and broad diffraction peaks in PXRD, which is in agree with the observations in other solid solutions.10
To investigate the distribution of linkers within CVMOF-DE, molecular modeling were conducted in combination with flurescence quenching experiments. Three possible structural models were proposed, in which LD and LE were alternatively arranged, randomly distributed, or formed clusters. It has been demonstrated that the distributions of two linkers within a mixed-linker MOFs can be determined by Förster resonance energy transfer (FRET)-based flurescence quenching.23 LE is able to quench the flurescence of LD by FRET (Figure S9a), and the quenching efficiency is strongly dependent on the distance of fluorophore-quencher pair.24-25 Therefore, different distributions of linkers are expected to result in various flurescence quenching behaviors. The alternating, random, and clustered structural model were built according to the method reported by Yaghi and coworkers.26 Generally, a $7.5{\times}7.5{\times}7.5$ superlattice of CVMOF-DE was built by abstracting LD and LE as two different lattice points. A Monte Carlo simulation is then conducted in which randomly selected linkers are interchanged, and the new structure is accepted or rejected according to the energy change. By adjusting the effective interaction energies between LD and LE (Table S5), the alternating, random, and clustered structural models were obtained (Figure 5a). The ratios of LE were increased from 0 to $35\%$ , and the modeling process iterated to get structural models of CVMOF-DE with different linker ratios. The fluorescence intensity as a function of LE (quencher) ratio was simulated for three structural models respectively (Supporting Information, Simulation Details). The random and alternating models show similar flurescence quenching behavior, which was completely quenched with only $2\%$ of LE presented. However, the flurescence intensity was largely maintained in the clustered model (Figures 5b). Indeed, if the LD and LE form discrete domains within the lattice, most of the fluorophores would be sufficiently far away from the quenchers and quenching would only occur at or near the interface between domains. Therefore, the alternating and random models match with the experimental fluorescence quenching. The application of flurescence quenching method is limited to CVMOF-DE which contains a fluorophore-quencher pair. Other techniques such as $^1\mathrm{H}$ -spin-diffusion spectroscopy have been reported to probe the distribution of linkers in MOFs,27 which could potentially be applied to study CVMOFs.
Structures of CVMOF-DE were simulated in a $3\times3\times3$ supercell using random and alternating models, respectively (Figure S10). The average unit cell edge lengths were optimized to be $27.64\AA$ for random model, which matched well with the Pawley refinement results of PXRD patterns ${\zeta}a=27.65$ Å). The simulated PXRD of CVMOF-DE based on random model showed broaden and weak high angle peaks, in consistence with the experimental data (Figures 5c and S11). Furthermore, the simulated pore size of CVMOF-DE based on random model lies in the interval of the pore sizes of MOF-D and MOF-E, in agreement with the pore size estimated by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption (Figure S12). For comparison, the alternating structural model of CVMOF-DE leads to additional peaks in PXRD due to the reduced symmetry (Figure 5c blue line). Experimental evidence and simulations indicated a random distribution of LD and LE within CVMOF-DE. Consequently, the overall structure of CVMOF-DE can be described as two linkers randomly distributed in a UiO-type of framework with one linker acting as substitutional defects of the other.
The substitutional defects of two linkers within CVMOFs are essentially different from the missing-linker defects commonly observed in many $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -MOFs. Missing linker defects are usually created by replacing linkers with terminal ligands, such as a pair of ${-}0\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ . To analyze the concentration of missinglinker defects in CVMOF-DE, diffuse reflectance infrared fourier transform spectra (DRIFTs) and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) were conducted. In the DRIFTs spectra of CVMOF-DE (Figure S12), a sharp single band of $\upmu_{3}–0\mathrm{H}$ was observed at $3674~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , while broad bands at ${\sim}3000~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were assigned to hydrogen-bonded ${-}0\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ at missing-linker defect sites. The intensity of $\upmu_{3}–0\mathrm{H}$ and ${-}0\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ bands were similar for MOF
D, CVMOF-DE, and MOF-E, suggesting that mixed-linker CVMOFs do not create additional linker deficiency. The TGA curves (Figure S13) demonstrated that the weight loss relative to theoretically expected ones are very similar among MOF-D, CVMOF-DE, and MOF-E, indicating the same linker deficiency. Based on the weight loss of linkers relevant to the final weight, the connection numbers of $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster were estimated to be ${\sim}10.5$ for MOF-D, CVMOF-DE, and MOF-E. These results indicated that the CVMOFs were as defective as single-linker MOFs in terms of missing-linker defects.
Versatility of CVMOFs. The synthesis of CVMOFs can be potentially applied to other MOF systems. To explore the versatility of our method, a mixture of LD and LE was used to synthesize Zn-based BioMOF-100,28 Zr-based hcp-UiO-67,29 and Ce-based Ce-UiO-67,30 respectively. The mixed-linker MOFs show similar shape of PXRD patterns as their respective parent structures with shifted peak positions (Figure S10), suggesting that uniform mixed-linker CVMOFs were formed in all the three structures. Considering the large number of available MOF structures, many more mixed-linker CVMOFs can be expected.
## CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, we have demonstrated that a mixture of linkers with different lengths can form uniform solid-solutions in a MOF. This method allows the precise control of lattice metrics and their related gas adsorption properties. The lattice engineering by adjusting the linker ratios opens the way to control the physical and chemical properties of MOFs. A greater exploration of this type of mixed-linker MOFs, either in respect of framework structures or unique physiochemical properties, will lead to new discoveries in MOF field and beyond. Potential applications of such materials include gas storage, separation, catalysis and chemical sensing.
